 a person changes how the world perceives them. General reference material on celebrity notes that fame comes from broad public recognition and that celebrities are often perceived as having glowing or idealized qualities, a halo-like effect. [2]","No published study has tested the theory under this name. Researchers on celebrity and attraction discuss halo effects more broadly. [2]"],"infobox":[["Type","Community theory / framework"],["Abbreviation","JBF"],["Core claim","Fame overrides facial rating"],["Related ideas","Halo effect, status, PSL ratings"]],"sections":[{"h":"Beliefs and claims","paras":["Believers cite pairings of publicly known men rated modestly on forum scales with highly rated partners as illustrations. [1] The theory also appears in discussions about how much of attraction is facial structure versus social proof."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Expert views","paras":["General sources describe fame as bringing a favorable public image and a tendency for the public to view celebrities as having above-average abilities. [2] No study cited here measures whether fame offsets facial attractiveness in dating outcomes."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Clinical studies","paras":["No studies found."],"subs":[]}],"lore":{"intro":"The idea circulates on looksmax.org as a shorthand argument in debates about whether looks or status matter more. [1]","claims":[{"claim":"Fame can override a low facial rating","verdict":"unknown","note":""}]},"timeline":[],"refs":[{"title":"just be famous theory 3.0 psl dude dating a supermodel","url":"https://looksmax.org/threads/just-be-famous-theory-3-0-psl-dude-dating-a-supermodel.55927/","source":"looksmax.org","year":null,"type":"forum"},{"title":"Celebrity (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Celebrity","source":"Wikipedia","year":null,"type":"encyclopedia"}],"studies":[],"reddit":[],"forums":[{"title":"Famous \"high value woman\" thewizardliz gets back with the guy who cheated on her while pregnant","url":"https://looksmax.org/threads/